  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers today into Monday morning. Rainfall \nwill have minimal impact on the drought.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure begins to develop in the Mid-Atlantic region and a\ncold front crosses Quebec Province today. Clouds will increase \nearly this morning, with showers then approaching from the west.\nThe low will reach the Gulf of Maine later today, while the \ncold front approaches the Saint Lawrence Valley. Models have \nbeen trending offshore with the better upper level support and \nbetter precipitable water values. Current indications are that \nthe heavier rains should remain across the waters of the Gulf of\nMaine, possibly clipping Downeast areas. Other showers are \npossible across northern areas in advance of the approaching \ncold front. Although instability is limited, could also have \nisolated/scattered thunderstorms across northern areas this\nafternoon/evening. The low exits across the Maritimes tonight, \nwhile the cold front crosses the region and an upper trof \napproaches from the west. Shower chances will persist across the\nregion tonight. The upper trof then crosses the region Monday \nkeeping a chance of mostly early showers. Rainfall with this \nsystem will have minimal impact on the drought, with only\naround a third of an inch expect for Downeast and less to the \nnorth.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Patchy/areas of frost possible across northern \nareas Monday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will build across the region behind the cold \nfront Monday, bringing clear skies and light winds. This will \nresult in favorable conditions for radiational cooling. Low \ntemperatures will range from the mid to upper 30s across much of\nnorthern Maine, with some locations in the North Woods cooling \ndown into the low 30s. Patchy/areas of frost are possible Monday\nnight across the northern areas, particularly in colder valleys. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Chance for rain showers by late next week.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3 DESCRIPTION...\nLow pressure system to the far north moves eastward, pushing a\nfront through New England late next week. Models disagree on \nthe timing of the pressure system, with the ECMWF generally \nmoving the system through the region by the end of the week, \nand the GFS moves the system through later into the weekend. Not\nanticipating enough rainfall to provide drought improvement at \nthis time.",
